<!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
<section class="ac-blue section" style="padding-bottom: 80px;">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Frequently Asked Questions</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Customer data questions, answered.</h2>
    </div>
    <div style="max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Is this a CDP, and how does it sit alongside our CRM?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>No. A customer data platform pulls in data from your website, from email tools such as Klaviyo and from other sources, and Teamwork Commerce does not do that. It holds the customer record used in store: profiles, preferences, purchase history and consent, captured through self-registration and tied to transactions at the point of sale. Open customer APIs import and export customer information, so it sits alongside the CRM you already run rather than replacing it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          How does customer sign-up work at the register?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Customers register themselves by scanning a QR code on their own device, so nobody types details at the cash wrap and the queue keeps moving. A scannable barcode ties the new profile to the transaction and receipt instantly, and email or SMS marketing consent is captured during registration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Does this replace our CRM?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>No. Open customer APIs import and export customer information, so it supports the CRM workflows you already run rather than requiring a full migration. Records are kept clean and free of duplicates, with languages, locations, and registration templates configured per market.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          What is the AI at the point of sale?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>It is an AI assistant built into the associate's handheld device, there to help associates help their customers. Instead of digging through binders or radioing the back room, an associate asks a question the way they would ask a colleague and gets an answer, with pictures, in seconds. They can describe what the customer wants in plain language (“something warm for a weekend in the mountains”), scan a tag to pull up product information, or browse by category to see what is new, then save and share products. Faster answers mean less time searching and more time selling, and customers leave with exactly what they came for, or something even better.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

